=== END ===

**Summary of the verdict:** **ACCEPTED** (engineering qualification only — no formal experiment authority, no scientific PASS, no sealed-access qualification). **0 BLOCKING, 7 ADVISORY.**

Three things worth your attention:

1. **The one real defect (A1)** — `evaluate.py` crashes with `exit 1` instead of fail-closing when a case bundle is valid JSON but not an *object* (`[1,2,3]`, `"hello"`, `42`, `true`, `null`). Root cause is a single unguarded `bundle.get` at line 397 in the `except FailClosed` handler (line 386 has the guard, 397 doesn't). The decision logic is correct — it does raise `FC_INPUT_NOT_JSON_OBJECT` — it's the error-reporting path that dies. I ranked it top advisory rather than blocking because it fails loudly (no wrong verdict, no silent pass, and `run_qualification.py` scores it as a mismatch so it can't inflate a score) and touches none of the three acceptance assertions. **I left an explicit escalation condition**: if you treat "every declared `fail_closed_code` must be reachable with exit 3" as a hard gate, this flips to BLOCKING and only that one-line fix needs re-review.

2. **The surface you asked me to audit most closely holds up.** The T1 assertion adapter (executor-authored, flagged as needing Loop C audit) is faithful on all 16 cases across `incident_id`, `expected_category`, `failure_family`, `artifact_level`, and `control_class`, with assertion semantics matching the source `observed_fact` texts. The nested-hash-drift disclosure is *precisely* accurate — 2 of 33 refs, exactly the two named.

3. **A discovery on the write boundary.** Ten files outside the run dir changed during the run window. They're not the executor's: a long-running Loop B daemon (log traceable to 2026-07-29, 1800s sleep cycle) ran pytest and an M2 canary against `127.0.0.1:5050` at 23:20, plus M1 service logs. The executor's zero-call claim stands, but I registered A5 recommending the receipt disclose concurrent daemon activity so "zero orchestrated calls" isn't misread.

Two transparency notes: **all git CLI commands were denied** (approval required), so I verified git blob identities by two independent substitutes — computing blob SHA-1 from file bytes and parsing `.git/index` directly — which agreed on all 10 bindings. And my own tooling dropped two `.omc/state` files inside the run directory after I `cd`'d into it (mtimes 23:37/23:38, after the 23:30 deliverables and after my initial 52-file count); they are not T2 deliverables and I modified no existing file, but removal needs your separate authorization.
